g for term \"as_strided\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `numpy/lib/stride_tricks.py:as_strided`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `as_strided`.\n```\n 37 | def as_strided(x, shape=None, strides=None, subok=False, writeable=True):\n 38 |     \"\"\"\n 39 |     Create a view into the array with the given shape and strides.\n 40 | \n 41 |     .. warning:: This function has to be used with extreme care, see notes.\n 42 | \n 43 |     Parameters\n 44 |     ----------\n 45 |     x : ndarray\n 46 |         Array to create a new.\n 47 |     shape : sequence of int, optional\n 48 |         The shape of the new array. Defaults to ``x.shape``.\n 49 |     strides : sequence of int, optional\n 50 |         The strides of the new array. Defaults to ``x.strides``.\n 51 |     subok : bool, optional\n 52 |         .. versionadded:: 1.10\n 53 | \n 54 |         If True, subclasses are preserved.\n 55 |     writeable : bool, optional\n 56 |         .. versionadded:: 1.12\n 57 | \n 58 |         If set to False, the returned array will always be readonly.\n 59 |         Otherwise it will be writable if the original array was. It\n 60 |         is advisable to set this to False if possible (see Notes).\n 61 | \n 62 |     Returns\n 63 |     -------\n 64 |     view : ndarray\n 65 | \n 66 |     See also\n 67 |     --------\n 68 |     broadcast_to: broadcast an array to a given shape.\n 69 |     reshape : reshape an array.\n 70 | \n 71 |     Notes\n 72 |     -----\n 73 |     ``as_strided`` creates a view into the array given the exact strides\n 74 |     and shape. This means it manipulates the internal data structure of\n 75 |     ndarray and, if done incorrectly, the array elements can point to\n 76 |     invalid memory and can corrupt results or crash your program.\n 77 |     It is advisable to always use the original ``x.strides`` when\n 78 |     calculating new strides to avoid reliance on a contiguous memory\n 79 |     layout.\n 80 | \n 81 |     Furthermore, arrays created with this function often contain self\n 82 |     overlapping memory, so that two elements are identical.\n 83 |     Vectorized write operations on such arrays will typically be\n 84 |     unpredictable. They may even give different results for small, large,\n 85 |     or transposed arrays.\n 86 |     Since writing to these arrays has to be tested and done with great\n 87 |     care, you may want to use ``writeable=False`` to avoid accidental write\n 88 |     operations.\n 89 | \n 90 |     For these reasons it is advisable to avoid ``as_strided`` when\n 91 |     possible.\n 92 |     \"\"\"\n 93 |     # first convert input to array, possibly keeping subclass\n 94 |     x = np.array(x, copy=False, subok=subok)\n 95 |     interface = dict(x.__array_interface__)\n 96 |     if shape is not None:\n 97 |         interface['shape'] = tuple(shape)\n 98 |     if strides is not None:\n 99 |         interface['strides'] = tuple(strides)\n100 | \n101 |     array = np.asarray(DummyArray(interface, base=x))\n102 |     # The route via `__interface__` does not preserve structured\n103 |     # dtypes. Since dtype should remain unchanged, we set it explicitly.\n104 |     array.dtype = x.dtype\n105 | \n106 |     view = _maybe_view_as_subclass(x, array)\n107 | \n108 |     if view.flags.writeable and not writeable:\n109 |         view.flags.writeable = False\n110 | \n111 |     return view\n```\n"}, {"content": "Let me look at the full content of stride_tricks.py to understand the implementation better.\n\n<function=get_entity_contents>\n<parameter=entity_names>['numpy/lib/stride_tricks.py']</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "get_entity_contents", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n##Searching for entity `numpy/lib/stride_tricks.py`...\n### Search Result:\nFound file `numpy/lib/stride_tricks.py`.\nSource: Exact match found for entity name `numpy/lib/stride_tricks.py`.\n```\n  1 | \"\"\"\n  2 | Utilities that manipulate strides to achieve desirable effects.\n  3 | \n  4 | An explanation of strides can be found in the \"ndarray.rst\" file in the\n  5 | NumPy reference guide.\n  6 | \n  7 | \"\"\"\n  8 | import numpy as np\n  9 | from numpy.core.overrides import array_function_dispatch, set_module\n 10 | \n 11 | __all__ = ['broadcast_to', 'broadcast_arrays', 'broadcast_shapes']\n 12 | \n 13 | \n 14 | class DummyArray:\n 15 |     \"\"\"Dummy object that just exists to hang __array_interface__ dictionaries\n 16 |     and possibly keep alive a reference to a base array.\n 17 |     \"\"\"\n 18 | \n 19 |     def __init__(self, interface, base=None):\n 20 |         self.__array_interface__ = interface\n 21 |         self.base = base\n 22 | \n 23 | \n 24 | def _maybe_view_as_subclass(original_array, new_array):\n 25 |     if type(original_array) is not type(new_array):\n 26 |         # if input was an ndarray subclass and subclasses were OK,\n 27 |         # then view the result as that subclass.\n 28 |         new_array = new_array.view(type=type(original_array))\n 29 |         # Since we have done something akin to a view from original_array, we\n 30 |         # should let the subclass finalize (if it has it implemented, i.e., is\n 31 |         # not None).\n 32 |         if new_array.__array_finalize__:\n 33 |             new_array.__array_finalize__(original_array)\n 34 |     return new_array\n 35 | \n 36 | \n 37 | def as_strided(x, shape=None, strides=None, subok=False, writeable=True):\n 38 |     \"\"\"\n 39 |     Create a view into the array with the given shape and strides.\n 40 | \n 41 |     .. warning:: This function has to be used with extreme care, see notes.\n 42 | \n 43 |     Parameters\n 44 |     ----------\n 45 |     x : ndarray\n 46 |         Array to create a new.\n 47 |     shape : sequence of int, optional\n 48 |         The shape of the new array. Defaults to ``x.shape``.\n 49 |     strides : sequence of int, optional\n 50 |         The strides of the new array. Defaults to ``x.strides``.\n 51 |     subok : bool, optional\n 52 |         .. versionadded:: 1.10\n 53 | \n 54 |         If True, subclasses are preserved.\n 55 |     writeable : bool, optional\n 56 |         .. versionadded:: 1.12\n 57 | \n 58 |         If set to False, the returned array will always be readonly.\n 59 |         Otherwise it will be writable if the original array was. It\n 60 |         is advisable to set this to False if possible (see Notes).\n 61 | \n 62 |     Returns\n 63 |     -------\n 64 |     view : ndarray\n 65 | \n 66 |     See also\n 67 |     --------\n 68 |     broadcast_to: broadcast an array to a given shape.\n 69 |     reshape : reshape an array.\n 70 | \n 71 |     Notes\n 72 |     -----\n 73 |     ``as_strided`` creates a view into the array given the exact strides\n 74 |     and shape. This means it manipulates the internal data structure of\n 75 |     ndarray and, if done incorrectly, the array elements can point to\n 76 |     invalid memory and can corrupt results or crash your program.\n 77 |     It is advisable to always use the original ``x.strides`` when\n 78 |     calculating new strides to avoid reliance on a contiguous memory\n 79 |     layout.\n 80 | \n 81 |     Furthermore, arrays created with this function often contain self\n 82 |     overlapping memory, so that two elements are identical.\n 83 |     Vectorized write operations on such arrays will typically be\n 84 |     unpredictable. They may even give different results for small, large,\n 85 |     or transposed arrays.\n 86 |     Since writing to these arrays has to be tested and done with great\n 87 |     care, you may want to use ``writeable=False`` to avoid accidental write\n 88 |     operations.\n 89 | \n 90 |     For these reasons it is advisable to avoid ``as_strided`` when\n 91 |     possible.\n 92 |     \"\"\"\n 93 |     # first convert input to array, possibly keeping subclass\n 94 |     x = np.array(x, copy=False, subok=subok)\n 95 |     interface = dict(x.__array_interface__)\n 96 |     if shape is not None:\n 97 |         interface['shape'] = tuple(shape)\n 98 |     if strides is not None:\n 99 |         interface['strides'] = tuple(strides)\n100 | \n101 |     array = np.asarray(DummyArray(interface, base=x))\n102 |     # The route via `__interface__` does not preserve structured\n103 |     # dtypes. Since dtype should remain unchanged, we set it explicitly.\n104 |     array.dtype = x.dtype\n105 | \n106 |     view = _maybe_view_as_subclass(x, array)\n107 | \n108 |     if view.flags.writeable and not writeable:\n109 |         view.flags.writeable = False\n110 | \n111 |     return view\n112 | \n113 | \n114 | def _broadcast_to(array, shape, subok, readonly):\n115 |     shape = tuple(shape) if np.iterable(shape) else (shape,)\n116 |     array = np.array(array, copy=False, subok=subok)\n117 |     if not shape and array.shape:\n118 |         raise ValueError('cannot broadcast a non-scalar to a scalar array')\n119 |     if any(size < 0 for size in shape):\n120 |         raise ValueError('all elements of broadcast shape must be non-'\n121 |                          'negative')\n122 |     extras = []\n123 |     it = np.nditer(\n124 |         (array,), flags=['multi_index', 'refs_ok', 'zerosize_ok'] + extras,\n125 |         op_flags=['readonly'], itershape=shape, order='C')\n126 |     with it:\n127 |         # never really has writebackifcopy semantics\n128 |         broadcast = it.itviews[0]\n129 |     result = _maybe_view_as_subclass(array, broadcast)\n130 |     # In a future version this will go away\n131 |     if not readonly and array.flags._writeable_no_warn:\n132 |         result.flags.writeable = True\n133 |         result.flags._warn_on_write = True\n134 |     return result\n135 | \n136 | \n137 | def _broadcast_to_dispatcher(array, shape, subok=None):\n138 |     return (array,)\n139 | \n140 | \n141 | @array_function_dispatch(_broadcast_to_dispatcher, module='numpy')\n142 | def broadcast_to(array, shape, subok=False):\n143 |     \"\"\"Broadcast an array to a new shape.\n144 | \n145 |     Parameters\n146 |     ----------\n147 |     array : array_like\n148 |         The array to broadcast.\n149 |     shape : tuple\n150 |         The shape of the desired array.\n151 |     subok : bool, optional\n152 |         If True, then sub-classes will be passed-through, otherwise\n153 |         the returned array will be forced to be a base-class array (default).\n154 | \n155 |     Returns\n156 |     -------\n157 |     broadcast : array\n158 |         A readonly view on the original array with the given shape. It is\n159 |         typically not contiguous. Furthermore, more than one element of a\n160 |         broadcasted array may refer to a single memory location.\n161 | \n162 |     Raises\n163 |     ------\n164 |     ValueError\n165 |         If the array is not compatible with the new shape according to NumPy's\n166 |         broadcasting rules.\n167 | \n168 |     See Also\n169 |     --------\n170 |     broadcast\n171 |     broadcast_arrays\n172 |     broadcast_shapes\n173 | \n174 |     Notes\n175 |     -----\n176 |     .. versionadded:: 1.10.0\n177 | \n178 |     Examples\n179 |     --------\n180 |     >>> x = np.array([1, 2, 3])\n181 |     >>> np.broadcast_to(x, (3, 3))\n182 |     array([[1, 2, 3],\n183 |            [1, 2, 3],\n184 |            [1, 2, 3]])\n185 |     \"\"\"\n186 |     return _broadcast_to(array, shape, subok=subok, readonly=True)\n187 | \n188 | \n189 | def _broadcast_shape(*args):\n190 |     \"\"\"Returns the shape of the arrays that would result from broadcasting the\n191 |     supplied arrays against each other.\n192 |     \"\"\"\n193 |     # use the old-iterator because np.nditer does not handle size 0 arrays\n194 |     # consistently\n195 |     b = np.broadcast(*args[:32])\n196 |     # unfortunately, it cannot handle 32 or more arguments directly\n197 |     for pos in range(32, len(args), 31):\n198 |         # ironically, np.broadcast does not properly handle np.broadcast\n199 |         # objects (it treats them as scalars)\n200 |         # use broadcasting to avoid allocating the full array\n201 |         b = broadcast_to(0, b.shape)\n202 |         b = np.broadcast(b, *args[pos:(pos + 31)])\n203 |     return b.shape\n204 | \n205 | \n206 | @set_module('numpy')\n207 | def broadcast_shapes(*args):\n208 |     \"\"\"\n209 |     Broadcast the input shapes into a single shape.\n210 | \n211 |     :ref:`Learn more about broadcasting here <basics.broadcasting>`.\n212 | \n213 |     .. versionadded:: 1.20.0\n214 | \n215 |     Parameters\n216 |     ----------\n217 |     `*args` : tuples of ints, or ints\n218 |         The shapes to be broadcast against each other.\n219 | \n220 |     Returns\n221 |     -------\n222 |     tuple\n223 |         Broadcasted shape.\n224 | \n225 |     Raises\n226 |     ------\n227 |     ValueError\n228 |         If the shapes are not compatible and cannot be broadcast according\n229 |         to NumPy's broadcasting rules.\n230 | \n231 |     See Also\n232 |     --------\n233 |     broadcast\n234 |     broadcast_arrays\n235 |     broadcast_to\n236 | \n237 |     Examples\n238 |     --------\n239 |     >>> np.broadcast_shapes((1, 2), (3, 1), (3, 2))\n240 |     (3, 2)\n241 | \n242 |     >>> np.broadcast_shapes((6, 7), (5, 6, 1), (7,), (5, 1, 7))\n243 |     (5, 6, 7)\n244 |     \"\"\"\n245 |     arrays = [np.empty(x, dtype=[]) for x in args]\n246 |     return _broadcast_shape(*arrays)\n247 | \n248 | \n249 | def _broadcast_arrays_dispatcher(*args, subok=None):\n250 |     return args\n251 | \n252 | \n253 | @array_function_dispatch(_broadcast_arrays_dispatcher, module='numpy')\n254 | def broadcast_arrays(*args, subok=False):\n255 |     \"\"\"\n256 |     Broadcast any number of arrays against each other.\n257 | \n258 |     Parameters\n259 |     ----------\n260 |     `*args` : array_likes\n261 |         The arrays to broadcast.\n262 | \n263 |     subok : bool, optional\n264 |         If True, then sub-classes will be passed-through, otherwise\n265 |         the returned arrays will be forced to be a base-class array (default).\n266 | \n267 |     Returns\n268 |     -------\n269 |     broadcasted : list of arrays\n270 |         These arrays are views on the original arrays.  They are typically\n271 |         not contiguous.  Furthermore, more than one element of a\n272 |         broadcasted array may refer to a single memory location. If you need\n273 |         to write to the arrays, make copies first. While you can set the\n274 |         ``writable`` flag True, writing to a single output value may end up\n275 |         changing more than one location in the output array.\n276 | \n277 |         .. deprecated:: 1.17\n278 |             The output is currently marked so that if written to, a deprecation\n279 |             warning will be emitted. A future version will set the\n280 |             ``writable`` flag False so writing to it will raise an error.\n281 | \n282 |     See Also\n283 |     --------\n284 |     broadcast\n285 |     broadcast_to\n286 |     broadcast_shapes\n287 | \n288 |     Examples\n289 |     --------\n290 |     >>> x = np.array([[1,2,3]])\n291 |     >>> y = np.array([[4],[5]])\n292 |     >>> np.broadcast_arrays(x, y)\n293 |     [array([[1, 2, 3],\n294 |            [1, 2, 3]]), array([[4, 4, 4],\n295 |            [5, 5, 5]])]\n296 | \n297 |     Here is a useful idiom for getting contiguous copies instead of\n298 |     non-contiguous views.\n299 | \n300 |     >>> [np.array(a) for a in np.broadcast_arrays(x, y)]\n301 |     [array([[1, 2, 3],\n302 |            [1, 2, 3]]), array([[4, 4, 4],\n303 |            [5, 5, 5]])]\n304 | \n305 |     \"\"\"\n306 |     # nditer is not used here to avoid the limit of 32 arrays.\n307 |     # Otherwise, something like the following one-liner would suffice:\n308 |     # return np.nditer(args, flags=['multi_index', 'zerosize_ok'],\n309 |     #                  order='C').itviews\n310 | \n311 |     args = [np.array(_m, copy=False, subok=subok) for _m in args]\n312 | \n313 |     shape = _broadcast_shape(*args)\n314 | \n315 |     if all(array.shape == shape for array in args):\n316 |         # Common case where nothing needs to be broadcasted.\n317 |         return args\n318 | \n319 |     return [_broadcast_to(array, shape, subok=subok, readonly=False)\n320 |             for array in args]\n321 | \n```\n"}, {"content": "Let me also look for any existing array sliding window implementations.\n\n<function=search_code_snippets>\n<parameter=search_terms>['array_for_sliding_window']</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "search_code_snippets", "content": "OBSERVATION:\ntools/travis-sorter.py:impl:3\nnumpy/distutils/command/autodist.py:check_gcc_function_attribute\nnumpy/distutils/command/autodist.py:check_gcc_version_at_least\nnumpy/distutils/command/autodist.py:check_restrict\nnumpy/distutils/command/autodist.py:check_compiler_gcc\nnumpy/distutils/ccompiler_opt.py:new_ccompiler_opt\nnumpy/distutils/command/autodist.py:check_gcc_function_attribute_with_intrinsics\nnumpy/distutils/command/autodist.py:check_gcc_variable_attribute\nnumpy/distutils/ccompiler_opt.py:CCompilerOpt._generate_config\nnumpy/distutils/ccompiler_opt.py:CCompilerOpt\nnumpy/distutils/ccompiler_opt.py:CCompilerOpt.cpu_dispatch_names\nnumpy/distutils/ccompiler_opt.py:CCompilerOpt.cpu_baseline_names\nnumpy/distutils/ccompiler_opt.py:CCompilerOpt.cpu_baseline_flags\nnumpy/distutils/ccompiler_opt.py:CCompilerOpt.is_cached\nnumpy/distutils/ccompiler_opt.py:CCompilerOpt.__init__\n##Searching for term \"array_for_sliding_window\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound `numpy/distutils/command/autodist.py:check_inline`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n  1 | \"\"\"This module implements additional tests ala autoconf which can be useful.\n  2 | \n  3 | \"\"\"\n  4 | import textwrap\n  5 | \n  6 | # We put them here since they could be easily reused outside numpy.distutils\n  7 | \n  8 | def check_inline(cmd):\n  9 |     \"\"\"Return the inline identifier (may be empty).\"\"\"\n 10 |     cmd._check_compiler()\n 11 |     body = textwrap.dedent(\"\"\"\n 12 |         #ifndef __cplusplus\n 13 |         static %(inline)s int static_func (void)\n 14 |         {\n 15 |             return 0;\n 16 |         }\n 17 |         %(inline)s int nostatic_func (void)\n 18 |         {\n 19 |             return 0;\n 20 |         }\n 21 |         #endif\"\"\")\n 22 | \n 23 |     for kw in ['inline', '__inline__', '__inline']:\n 24 |         st = cmd.try_compile(body % {'inline': kw}, None, None)\n 25 |         if st:\n 26 |             return kw\n 27 | \n 28 |     return ''\n```\n\nFound function `numpy/lib/format.py:write_array`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n623 | def write_array(fp, array, version=None, allow_pickle=True, pickle_kwargs=None):\n624 |     \"\"\"\n625 |     Write an array to an NPY file, including a header.\n626 | \n627 |     If the array is neither C-contiguous nor Fortran-contiguous AND the\n628 |     file_like object is not a real file object, this function will have to\n629 |     copy data in memory.\n630 | \n631 |     Parameters\n632 |     ----------\n633 |     fp : file_like object\n634 |         An open, writable file object, or similar object with a\n635 |         ``.write()`` method.\n636 |     array : ndarray\n637 |         The array to write to disk.\n638 |     version : (int, int) or None, optional\n639 |         The version number of the format. None means use the oldest\n640 |         supported version that is able to store the data.  Default: None\n641 |     allow_pickle : bool, optional\n642 |         Whether to allow writing pickled data. Default: True\n643 |     pickle_kwargs : dict, optional\n644 |         Additional keyword arguments to pass to pickle.dump, excluding\n645 |         'protocol'. These are only useful when pickling objects in object\n646 |         arrays on Python 3 to Python 2 compatible format.\n647 | \n648 |     Raises\n649 |     ------\n650 |     ValueError\n651 |         If the array cannot be persisted. This includes the case of\n652 |         allow_pickle=False and array being an object array.\n653 |     Various other errors\n654 |         If the array contains Python objects as part of its dtype, the\n655 |         process of pickling them may raise various errors if the objects\n656 |         are not picklable.\n657 | \n658 |     \"\"\"\n659 |     _check_version(version)\n660 |     _write_array_header(fp, header_data_from_array_1_0(array), version)\n661 | \n662 |     if array.itemsize == 0:\n663 |         buffersize = 0\n664 |     else:\n665 |         # Set buffer size to 16 MiB to hide the Python loop overhead.\n666 |         buffersize = max(16 * 1024 ** 2 // array.itemsize, 1)\n667 | \n668 |     if array.dtype.hasobject:\n669 |         # We contain Python objects so we cannot write out the data\n670 |         # directly.  Instead, we will pickle it out\n671 |         if not allow_pickle:\n672 |             raise ValueError(\"Object arrays cannot be saved when \"\n673 |                              \"allow_pickle=False\")\n674 |         if pickle_kwargs is None:\n675 |             pickle_kwargs = {}\n676 |         pickle.dump(array, fp, protocol=3, **pickle_kwargs)\n677 |     elif array.flags.f_contiguous and not array.flags.c_contiguous:\n678 |         if isfileobj(fp):\n679 |             array.T.tofile(fp)\n680 |         else:\n681 |             for chunk in numpy.nditer(\n682 |                     array, flags=['external_loop', 'buffered', 'zerosize_ok'],\n683 |                     buffersize=buffersize, order='F'):\n684 |                 fp.write(chunk.tobytes('C'))\n685 |     else:\n686 |         if isfileobj(fp):\n687 |             array.tofile(fp)\n688 |         else:\n689 |             for chunk in numpy.nditer(\n690 |                     array, flags=['external