l for obtaining a numeric representation of an\n665 |     array when all that matters is identifying distinct values. `factorize`\n666 |     is available as both a top-level function :func:`pandas.factorize`,\n667 |     and as a method :meth:`Series.factorize` and :meth:`Index.factorize`.\n668 | \n669 |     Parameters\n670 |     ----------\n671 |     {values}{sort}\n672 |     use_na_sentinel : bool, default True\n673 |         If True, the sentinel -1 will be used for NaN values. If False,\n674 |         NaN values will be encoded as non-negative integers and will not drop the\n675 |         NaN from the uniques of the values.\n676 | \n677 |         .. versionadded:: 1.5.0\n678 |     {size_hint}\\\n679 | \n680 |     Returns\n681 |     -------\n682 |     codes : ndarray\n683 |         An integer ndarray that's an indexer into `uniques`.\n684 |         ``uniques.take(codes)`` will have the same values as `values`.\n685 |     uniques : ndarray, Index, or Categorical\n686 |         The unique valid values. When `values` is Categorical, `uniques`\n687 |         is a Categorical. When `values` is some other pandas object, an\n688 |         `Index` is returned. Otherwise, a 1-D ndarray is returned.\n689 | \n690 |         .. note::\n691 | \n692 |            Even if there's a missing value in `values`, `uniques` will\n693 |            *not* contain an entry for it.\n694 | \n695 |     See Also\n696 |     --------\n697 |     cut : Discretize continuous-valued array.\n698 |     unique : Find the unique value in an array.\n699 | \n700 |     Notes\n701 |     -----\n702 |     Reference :ref:`the user guide <reshaping.factorize>` for more examples.\n703 | \n704 |     Examples\n705 |     --------\n706 |     These examples all show factorize as a top-level method like\n707 |     ``pd.factorize(values)``. The results are identical for methods like\n708 |     :meth:`Series.factorize`.\n709 | \n710 |     >>> codes, uniques = pd.factorize(np.array(['b', 'b', 'a', 'c', 'b'], dtype=\"O\"))\n711 |     >>> codes\n712 |     array([0, 0, 1, 2, 0])\n713 |     >>> uniques\n714 |     array(['b', 'a', 'c'], dtype=object)\n715 | \n716 |     With ``sort=True``, the `uniques` will be sorted, and `codes` will be\n717 |     shuffled so that the relationship is the maintained.\n718 | \n719 |     >>> codes, uniques = pd.factorize(np.array(['b', 'b', 'a', 'c', 'b'], dtype=\"O\"),\n720 |     ...                               sort=True)\n721 |     >>> codes\n722 |     array([1, 1, 0, 2, 1])\n723 |     >>> uniques\n724 |     array(['a', 'b', 'c'], dtype=object)\n725 | \n726 |     When ``use_na_sentinel=True`` (the default), missing values are indicated in\n727 |     the `codes` with the sentinel value ``-1`` and missing values are not\n728 |     included in `uniques`.\n729 | \n730 |     >>> codes, uniques = pd.factorize(np.array(['b', None, 'a', 'c', 'b'], dtype=\"O\"))\n731 |     >>> codes\n732 |     array([ 0, -1,  1,  2,  0])\n733 |     >>> uniques\n734 |     array(['b', 'a', 'c'], dtype=object)\n735 | \n736 |     Thus far, we've only factorized lists (which are internally coerced to\n737 |     NumPy arrays). When factorizing pandas objects, the type of `uniques`\n738 |     will differ. For Categoricals, a `Categorical` is returned.\n739 | \n740 |     >>> cat = pd.Categorical(['a', 'a', 'c'], categories=['a', 'b', 'c'])\n741 |     >>> codes, uniques = pd.factorize(cat)\n742 |     >>> codes\n743 |     array([0, 0, 1])\n744 |     >>> uniques\n745 |     ['a', 'c']\n746 |     Categories (3, object): ['a', 'b', 'c']\n747 | \n748 |     Notice that ``'b'`` is in ``uniques.categories``, despite not being\n749 |     present in ``cat.values``.\n750 | \n751 |     For all other pandas objects, an Index of the appropriate type is\n752 |     returned.\n753 | \n754 |     >>> cat = pd.Series(['a', 'a', 'c'])\n755 |     >>> codes, uniques = pd.factorize(cat)\n756 |     >>> codes\n757 |     array([0, 0, 1])\n758 |     >>> uniques\n759 |     Index(['a', 'c'], dtype='object')\n760 | \n761 |     If NaN is in the values, and we want to include NaN in the uniques of the\n762 |     values, it can be achieved by setting ``use_na_sentinel=False``.\n763 | \n764 |     >>> values = np.array([1, 2, 1, np.nan])\n765 |     >>> codes, uniques = pd.factorize(values)  # default: use_na_sentinel=True\n766 |     >>> codes\n767 |     array([ 0,  1,  0, -1])\n768 |     >>> uniques\n769 |     array([1., 2.])\n770 | \n771 |     >>> codes, uniques = pd.factorize(values, use_na_sentinel=False)\n772 |     >>> codes\n773 |     array([0, 1, 0, 2])\n774 |     >>> uniques\n775 |     array([ 1.,  2., nan])\n776 |     \"\"\"\n777 |     # Implementation notes: This method is responsible for 3 things\n778 |     # 1.) coercing data to array-like (ndarray, Index, extension array)\n779 |     # 2.) factorizing codes and uniques\n780 |     # 3.) Maybe boxing the uniques in an Index\n781 |     #\n782 |     # Step 2 is dispatched to extension types (like Categorical). They are\n783 |     # responsible only for factorization. All data coercion, sorting and boxing\n784 |     # should happen here.\n785 |     if isinstance(values, (ABCIndex, ABCSeries)):\n786 |         return values.factorize(sort=sort, use_na_sentinel=use_na_sentinel)\n787 | \n788 |     values = _ensure_arraylike(values, func_name=\"factorize\")\n789 |     original = values\n790 | \n791 |     if (\n792 |         isinstance(values, (ABCDatetimeArray, ABCTimedeltaArray))\n793 |         and values.freq is not None\n794 |     ):\n795 |         # The presence of 'freq' means we can fast-path sorting and know there\n796 |         #  aren't NAs\n797 |         codes, uniques = values.factorize(sort=sort)\n798 |         return codes, uniques\n799 | \n800 |     elif not isinstance(values, np.ndarray):\n801 |         # i.e. ExtensionArray\n802 |         codes, uniques = values.factorize(use_na_sentinel=use_na_sentinel)\n803 | \n804 |     else:\n805 |         values = np.asarray(values)  # convert DTA/TDA/MultiIndex\n806 | \n807 |  